{"reference":"Genesis 50","book":"Genesis","chapter":50,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"historical_context","title":"Genesis ends in Egypt","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Genesis closes with Joseph's death in Egypt while retaining the expectation that God will bring Abraham's family to the promised land. The ending therefore leaves the family in Egypt without presenting Egypt as the story's final destination.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_genesis_050_98b362a4e0b4e6cdd31c22d2"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"The larger movement of Genesis","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"The larger literary movement of Genesis shifts from universal beginnings in chapters 1–11 to the ancestral family narratives in chapters 12–50, ending with Jacob's household in Egypt. This conclusion gathers Genesis 50 into that larger movement.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_genesis_050_10018e5af3867d0667520893"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}
